WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
- Some people in the
Stapleton neighborhood

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:03.960
say what's happening over at
Dick's Sporting Goods Park

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.990
is not music to their ears.

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:08.820
They say that two day Bassnectar concert

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.620
literally rattled their
homes late into the night.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.560
- At one point dubstep was so big

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.940
that Colorado residents thought

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.840
a nearby Bassnectar
concert was an earthquake.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.460
- Thought it was an earthquake.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:22.910
I went outside and I looked,
and realized it was bass.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:23.929
- We have to be neighbors

00:00:23.929 --> 00:00:25.540
and we have to be friendly neighbors.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.010
This was not a friendly event.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:28.910
- Dubstep shook the landscape of music

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:31.200
in the early 2010s, but its viral success

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.410
was followed by its viral disappearance.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.630
I'm gonna try to make
my own dubstep track,

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.490
but first let's find out
where dubstep came from

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.640
and why it disappeared.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.700
The history of dubstep starts in

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.220
the UK garage music scene of the 90s.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.380
UK garage music came from house music.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.700
And features a four-on-the-floor
bass drum pattern

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.031
and syncopated high hats and snares.

00:00:53.031 --> 00:00:58.031
♪ In my dream you and
I are holding there ♪

00:00:58.210 --> 00:00:59.970
DJs took the syncopation in garage

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.760
a step further with a
sub-genre called 2-step.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.410
In 1999 music journalist Simon Reynolds

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:07.930
described 2-step as, a general rubric

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.220
for all kinds of jittery irregular rhythms

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.750
that don't conform to garage's

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.940
traditional four-to-the-floor pulse.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.000
Essentially, 2-step skipped over some of

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.650
the four-on-the-floor bass drum pattern

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:19.350
and added other shuffle percussion

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:20.950
to create a more erratic feel.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:23.740
Check out the off-kilter
pulse in this 2-step remix

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:24.984
featuring the singer Sia.

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:26.817
♪ If they fall away ♪

00:01:26.817 --> 00:01:28.315
♪ I'll stay with you ♪

00:01:28.315 --> 00:01:30.260
♪ Do as I say ♪

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:31.610
♪ Not as I do ♪

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:34.860
So 2-step is where the
step in dubstep comes from.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.010
But what about the dub?

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.890
Dub originated in Jamaica in the late 60s.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.370
Artists like King Tubby
reworked reggae instrumentals

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.310
making drums the focus of the tracks,

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.733
by raising the reverb, delay, and bass.

00:01:46.975 --> 00:01:49.585
♪ I never, I never never heard ♪

00:01:49.585 --> 00:01:50.857
♪ I never heard ♪

00:01:50.857 --> 00:01:52.982
♪ I never heard just some people ♪

00:01:52.982 --> 00:01:53.815
♪ No, no ♪

00:01:53.815 --> 00:01:54.660
Now hold on.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.290
Before you get angry in the comments,

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:58.810
I'm not saying dubstep came from Jamaica.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.270
But the way dub artists
altered reggae instrumentals

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.850
in the 60s inspired DJs in the late 90s

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.890
to rework 2-step tracks, creating dubstep.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:10.040
As dubstep pioneer Kode9 told Vice,

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:11.450
the name made sense.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:14.290
There were aspects of dub
that influenced dubstep.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:15.760
The most important was playing

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.360
the instrumental versions
of vocal garage tracks,

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.800
which was a little like
what dub was to reggae.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.450
The second was dub as a methodology,

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.150
manipulating sound to create
impossible sonic spaces

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:28.770
using reverb, echo, and such.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.280
This early dubstep was more minimal

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.890
than the dubstep we know today.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:33.270
But this minimalism provided room

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.830
for variation in the future.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.040
Check out this early
dubstep track by Burial,

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.703
an artist on Kode9's
record label, Hyperdub.

00:02:39.703 --> 00:02:41.543
♪ Holding you ♪

00:02:41.543 --> 00:02:43.123
♪ Couldn't be alone ♪

00:02:43.123 --> 00:02:44.821
♪ Couldn't be alone ♪

00:02:44.821 --> 00:02:45.654
♪ Loving you ♪

00:02:45.654 --> 00:02:48.200
A breakthrough in dubstep
came in the early 2000s

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.020
thanks to two teenage DJs from
England, Skream and Benga.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:53.630
Along with other London producers

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.039
they departed from the
dark sound of early dubstep

00:02:56.039 --> 00:02:58.280
by incorporating elements of reggae,

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.482
house, funk, and hip hop.

00:03:00.482 --> 00:03:03.399
(electronic music)

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:09.900
This more melodic sound found
an audience outside of London.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.870
And with the help of online
forums, spread around the world.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:14.750
The main ingredients
are syncopated rhythms

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:18.140
with a tempo between 138 and 142 BPM,

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:21.600
a kick on the one, and a
clap or snare on the three.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.686
But the defining ingredient
is that wobbling bassline.

00:03:24.686 --> 00:03:28.160
(electronic music)

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:29.730
The wobble effect is usually created

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.260
by modulating the bassline

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.060
with a low frequency oscillator, or LFO.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:35.900
- [Instructor] So now that
we got one brought up--

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:37.270
- I'm the original YouTube student.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.230
- Go ahead and create the
dubstep wobble thumbscratch

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:41.063
so we can use one of--

00:03:41.063 --> 00:03:43.220
- Look at that, I'm right here.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:44.053
And what he do?

00:03:44.053 --> 00:03:45.468
He did this one.

00:03:45.468 --> 00:03:47.580
(buzzing)

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.390
Y'all bear with me, don't trip
'cause we about to get it.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.252
- [Announcer] 20 minutes later.

00:03:52.252 --> 00:03:53.680
(buzzing)

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:54.620
Ooh.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.150
Listen for the wobbling bassline

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:58.628
in the song Midnight
Request Line by Skream.

00:03:58.628 --> 00:04:01.545
(electronic music)

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:08.380
By 2009, dubstep had taken
hold in the United States.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.260
And artists like Skrillex and Bassnectar

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.190
were creating their own
versions of the genre

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:15.240
by putting an emphasis on the
buildup and the bass drop.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.680
The bass drop consists of
dropping out all percussion

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.270
until near silence and
then resuming the song

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:22.163
with a heavy sub bass wobble.

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:27.560
- Oh my god!
(electronic music)

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:28.890
- This new version of dubstep

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:31.180
earned the mocking term brostep

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:34.200
due to its more aggressive
fist-bumping sound.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.220
What was once the
soundtrack for illegal raves

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.930
in London was now being played

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:40.323
in halftime shows and
in cereal commercials.

00:04:40.323 --> 00:04:44.010
(electronic music)

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:46.650
- [Announcer] New Weetabix
Chocolate Spoonsize.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:49.010
- Dubstep found its star moment in 2012

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:51.430
when Skrillex won three
awards at the Grammys.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:53.563
- Hi, Sonny Moore, Skrillex.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:56.310
This is really crazy for me man.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.980
Just a year and a half ago

00:04:57.980 --> 00:04:59.910
I was making that song in my bedroom.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:01.793
- Soon after, pop heavyweights
like Justin Bieber

00:05:01.793 --> 00:05:03.880
and Britney Spears began to include

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:05.750
more electronic sound in their work

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:08.372
as dubstep merged deeper
into the mainstream.

00:05:08.372 --> 00:05:10.368
♪ As long as you la la la la ♪

00:05:10.368 --> 00:05:12.099
♪ La la la la la ♪

00:05:12.099 --> 00:05:13.840
♪ La la la la la ♪

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.850
The DIY element of electronic music

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:18.180
in the early 2010s made it accessible,

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.670
but it also drew criticism
from music purists.

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.687
Computer programs like Ableton
with its presets and plugins

00:05:23.687 --> 00:05:27.280
allowed anyone with a laptop
to produce electronic music.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.910
Websites like Dubstepforum,
where audio programs

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.100
were shared and pirated, helped amateurs

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.600
break into the genre at
a professional level.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.640
Eventually artists reacted against

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:38.650
the in your face sound of dubstep.

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:40.040
The extreme nature of the music

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:41.710
that originally made it appealing

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:43.400
might be what pushed fans away.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.800
By 2013, dubstep started
to break into other genres.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.300
Post-dubstep artists, such as James Blake

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.130
and Mount Kimbie,
emerged as early as 2011.

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:54.130
And featured songs with
a slowed down tempo

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:56.614
in the range of 130 beats per minute.

00:05:56.614 --> 00:05:58.256
♪ That you will ever know ♪

00:05:58.256 --> 00:06:01.654
♪ The limitations of a clone ♪

00:06:01.654 --> 00:06:04.200
♪ They kept me on my throne ♪

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.870
By 2016 the popularity
of dubstep had declined.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:10.880
But it didn't disappear,
it just transformed.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:15.280
Dubstep mixed with Atlanta
trap to create EDM trap,

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:16.750
which is still popular today.

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:20.110
Other spinoff genres
include filth, glitch,

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:24.670
halfstep, chillstep,
deathstep, and drumstep.

00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:26.270
Even the pioneers of dubstep

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:28.430
began to distance
themselves from the genre.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:31.060
Around 2012, Skream began playing music

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:34.496
that was more informed by
house, disco, and techno.

00:06:34.496 --> 00:06:37.413
(electronic music)

00:06:41.391 --> 00:06:43.558
(buzzing)

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:48.020
I need it to be better than that though.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.530
It gotta be like.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:50.649
- [Instructor] And we're
gonna move this down

00:06:50.649 --> 00:06:51.993
to the sine wave.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.953
And we're gonna hit control click over it.

00:06:55.912 --> 00:06:58.070
Select number 14.

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:00.300
- Ooh, what you talking about?

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:01.833
- [Announcer] Two hours later.

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:04.337
- This the bassline right here.

00:07:04.337 --> 00:07:06.504
(singing)

00:07:08.612 --> 00:07:09.890
It's a sliding bassline.

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:10.886
Like this right here.

00:07:10.886 --> 00:07:13.053
(buzzing)

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:19.350
That's the bassline.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.981
And then the melody would be this.

00:07:21.981 --> 00:07:25.148
(buzzing and beeping)

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.100
I took different elements of dubstep,

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.830
different ingredients from
the list that we said earlier,

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:36.650
and I just made it my own.

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:40.460
I put it at 140 bpm, I
used the wobble bassline,

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.650
but I used it in the
beginning, not in the ending.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:44.420
Made the wobble a part of the buildup,

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:47.470
drop out, and then the full
instrumentation at the end.

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:48.610
I think it's a cool switch.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.018
I hope you guys enjoy it.

00:07:50.018 --> 00:07:52.935
(electronic music)

00:08:51.709 --> 00:08:52.934
That's tough.

00:08:52.934 --> 00:08:55.184
(singing)

